.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/escalade Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "escalade [![CI](https://github.com/lukeed/escalade/workflows/CI/badge.svg)](https://github.com/lukeed/escalade/actions) [![licenses](https://licenses.dev/b/npm/escalade)](https://licenses.dev/npm/escalade) [![codecov](https://badgen.now.sh/codecov/c/github/lukeed/escalade)](https://codecov.io/gh/lukeed/escalade)"
.PP
.RS 4
A tiny (183B to 210B) and \fBfast\fP utility to ascend parent directories 
.RE
.PP

.PP
With \fRescalade\fP, you can scale parent directories until you've found what you're looking for\&.
.br
Given an input file or directory, \fRescalade\fP will continue executing your callback function until either:

.PP
1) the callback returns a truthy value 2) \fRescalade\fP has reached the system root directory (eg, \fR/\fP)

.PP
.RS 4
\fBImportant:\fP
.br
Please note that \fRescalade\fP only deals with direct ancestry – it will not dive into parents' sibling directories\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP

.PP
\fBNotice:\fP As of v3\&.1\&.0, \fRescalade\fP now includes \fRDeno support\fP! Please see \fBDeno Usage\fP below\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save escalade
.fi
.PP
.SH "Modes"
.PP
There are two "versions" of \fRescalade\fP available:
.SS ""async""
.RS 4
\fBNode\&.js:\fP >= 8\&.x
.br
 \fBSize (gzip):\fP 210 bytes
.br
 \fBAvailability:\fP \fRCommonJS\fP, \fRES Module\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
This is the primary/default mode\&. It makes use of \fRasync\fP/\fRawait\fP and \fR\fRutil\&.promisify\fP\fP\&.
.SS ""sync""
.RS 4
\fBNode\&.js:\fP >= 6\&.x
.br
 \fBSize (gzip):\fP 183 bytes
.br
 \fBAvailability:\fP \fRCommonJS\fP, \fRES Module\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
This is the opt-in mode, ideal for scenarios where \fRasync\fP usage cannot be supported\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
\fI\fBExample Structure\fP\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
/Users/lukeed
  └── oss
    ├── license
    └── escalade
      ├── package\&.json
      └── test
        └── fixtures
          ├── index\&.js
          └── foobar
            └── demo\&.js
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fI\fBExample Usage\fP\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
//~> demo\&.js
import { join } from 'path';
import escalade from 'escalade';

const input = join(__dirname, 'demo\&.js');
// or: const input = __dirname;

const pkg = await escalade(input, (dir, names) => {
  console\&.log('~> dir:', dir);
  console\&.log('~> names:', names);
  console\&.log('\-\-\-');

  if (names\&.includes('package\&.json')) {
    // will be resolved into absolute
    return 'package\&.json';
  }
});

//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test/fixtures/foobar
//~> names: ['demo\&.js']
//\-\-\-
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test/fixtures
//~> names: ['index\&.js', 'foobar']
//\-\-\-
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test
//~> names: ['fixtures']
//\-\-\-
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade
//~> names: ['package\&.json', 'test']
//\-\-\-

console\&.log(pkg);
//=> /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/package\&.json

// Now search for "missing123\&.txt"
// (Assume it doesn't exist anywhere!)
const missing = await escalade(input, (dir, names) => {
  console\&.log('~> dir:', dir);
  return names\&.includes('missing123\&.txt') && 'missing123\&.txt';
});

//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test/fixtures/foobar
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test/fixtures
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade/test
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss/escalade
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed/oss
//~> dir: /Users/lukeed
//~> dir: /Users
//~> dir: /

console\&.log(missing);
//=> undefined
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fP To run the above example with "sync" mode, import from \fRescalade/sync\fP and remove the \fRawait\fP keyword\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "escalade(input, callback)"
Returns: \fRstring|void\fP or \fRPromise<string|void>\fP

.PP
When your \fRcallback\fP locates a file, \fRescalade\fP will resolve/return with an absolute path\&.
.br
 If your \fRcallback\fP was never satisfied, then \fRescalade\fP will resolve/return with nothing (undefined)\&.

.PP
.RS 4
\fBImportant:\fP
.br
The \fRsync\fP and \fRasync\fP versions share the same API\&.
.br
The \fBonly\fP difference is that \fRsync\fP is not Promise-based\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "input"
Type: \fRstring\fP

.PP
The path from which to start ascending\&.

.PP
This may be a file or a directory path\&.
.br
However, when \fRinput\fP is a file, \fRescalade\fP will begin with its parent directory\&.

.PP
.RS 4
\fBImportant:\fP Unless given an absolute path, \fRinput\fP will be resolved from \fRprocess\&.cwd()\fP location\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "callback"
Type: \fRFunction\fP

.PP
The callback to execute for each ancestry level\&. It always is given two arguments:

.PP
1) \fRdir\fP - an absolute path of the current parent directory 2) \fRnames\fP - a list (\fRstring[]\fP) of contents \fIrelative to\fP the \fRdir\fP parent

.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fP The \fRnames\fP list can contain names of files \fIand\fP directories\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
When your callback returns a \fIfalsey\fP value, then \fRescalade\fP will continue with \fRdir\fP's parent directory, re-invoking your callback with new argument values\&.

.PP
When your callback returns a string, then \fRescalade\fP stops iteration immediately\&.
.br
 If the string is an absolute path, then it's left as is\&. Otherwise, the string is resolved into an absolute path \fIfrom\fP the \fRdir\fP that housed the satisfying condition\&.

.PP
.RS 4
\fBImportant:\fP Your \fRcallback\fP can be a \fRPromise/AsyncFunction\fP when using the "async" version of \fRescalade\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
